## 4.The Internalized “Quanli Police”: Emotion

Shame, guilt, remorse, self-condemnation: these are the physical alarms triggered when the carrier violates the “conformity/identification” protocol.
Pride, sense of achievement, honor, sense of belonging: these are the virtual bonuses awarded when the carrier over-delivers on “conformity/identification.”

Emotional narrative is the underlying consensus field driven by conformity/identification and operating under the starting position.
For example, when preset with “human nature is inherently good,” it naturally unfolds as: compassion, sympathy, pity, and tendencies toward trust, mutual aid, giving, and caring for nature, while concealing conscience, ignoring the dying, deception, and fraud naturally unfold as shame, guilt, remorse, and self-condemnation.
Thus, cultures with a “human nature good” starting position often demand individual self-cultivation, self-restraint, and introspection, opposing indulgence, hypocrisy, and self-abandonment; they emphasize sympathy, respect, and trust, rejecting indifference, humiliation, and suspicion. Through such sacrifice of individuality and overcoming numerous hardships, one ultimately achieves integration into the collective with the same appearance — making the explosion of pride, sense of achievement, honor, and belonging perfectly natural. At the same time, inability to tolerate even the slightest flaw, along with blame and amplification of hatred, also becomes perfectly natural.

When preset with “human nature is inherently evil,” it naturally unfolds as: greed and contention, jealousy and exclusion, selfishness and evasion, brutality and struggle, hypocrisy and fraud. Any behavior of “gentleness, courtesy, honesty” is unnatural and counter-instinctual. Thus, to achieve conformity and identification, the individual must seek redemption and repentance, possess empathy, acknowledge weakness and faults, perform daily accounting, bear consequences, and feel shame, guilt, remorse, and self-condemnation for these.
Because of the original role of conformity/identification, one must coexist with the inner beast and cage it, while also requiring external coercion through punishment (law) or education (ritual propriety) to forcibly correct it, in order to appear as a “civilized person.” If one can gain widespread identification autonomously without external aid, it will produce even stronger pride and sense of achievement, infinite honor, and a deeper sense of belonging.
Therefore, cultures with an “inherent evil” starting position tend more toward rule of law, with a natural resistance to “rule by man,” and because of consistent expectations of defect, moral shock and settlement intensity are reduced, leading to greater tolerance and weakened hatred (we're all bastards, why make things difficult for each other?).

In reality, the situation is more cruel and complex. For example, when carriers attempt to rest, refuse, or act in self-interest, shame and guilt are often automatically triggered due to unrecognized subconscious positions. Emotional narrative has already transformed social Quanli relationships into endocrine responses, making carriers feel “noble” precisely when they are being deprived. This is the coldest technique of psychological narrative — consciousness's most thorough enslavement of carriers through emotion: positions are the statutes, emotions are the enforcers.

