## Summary

Psychological narrative is the eternal foundation of social structure. All grand narratives—myths, religions, morality, ethnicity, progress, capital, technology—are built upon the foundation of psychological narrative.

The essence of psychological narrative is consciousness's continuous meaning assignment to carriers, making carriers willing to pay the settlement for the story. For consciousness, this settlement is merely telling a story; for carriers, it is real physical depletion.

Conformity/identification serves as the “valve” through which consciousness drives carriers in narrative. Presets of human nature good/evil, nationalism, and survivorship bias narratives are all efficient ways consciousness operates this valve.

Society is not built on land or money, but on the “pre-occupation” of the carrier's psychological space. What is called civilization is a story in this psychological space where consciousness continuously issues credit, carriers continuously pay the price, yet feel “noble” in the illusion of emotion.

When psychological narrative loses credit (as seen in contemporary low desire, widespread depression, and identification crises), and when technological acceleration (capability) causes consciousness-issued credit (narrative) to exceed the carrier's bearing limit, the foundation of the entire social structure will shake. For humanity, psychological narrative has already gone through a long phase of practice and experimentation. Today, with technological acceleration, it has become necessary to re-examine and confront the carrier's settlement capacity.

